SRC/pdksh -- pdksh 4.3, OS-9 port edition 11, as rebuilt 2026-09-27=====================================================================CMDS/ksh was the porter's own binary until 2026-09-27.  It is now builtfrom this tree with the Microware C compiler of a later SDK, linkedtrap-free (cc -qm): it carries its own C library and no longer needs`cio'.  tools/rebuild/recipes.psv has the two lines that build it.Two source files differ from the port, and the port's copies are keptbeside them as c_sh.c.ori and io.c.ori.sh/c_sh.c, c_read() -- the `read' built-in, two faults------------------------------------------------------1. Only the first `while read ...; done < file' in a script read its   file.  read takes characters with getc(stdin), and stdin kept the   end-of-file flag the first loop left on it, so every later loop ended   at once.  c_read now calls clearerr() before it reads.2. A run of spaces counted as several separators: `read x y z' on   `a    b  c' gave x=a, y empty and z="   b  c".  Leading IFS white   space (space and tab) is now skipped before each field, as sh does.   The last name still takes the rest of the line as it stands.sh/io.c, fopenshf() -- one FILE per path----------------------------------------The shell opens its own FILE on paths 0 to 2.  pdksh frees the Clibrary's FILE for the same path first, so there is only one; the OSKport left that out.  With this C library, exit() closes path 1 throughthe first FILE and then cannot flush the second, so the output of abuilt-in in a pipeline -- `echo x | sed ...' -- was lost.  The OSK portnow frees it too.Building with the later SDK -- build-level changes only-------------------------------------------------------The port was built against its author's own headers and libraries.Against the SDK used here each of these was needed, and none changeswhat the shell does:  OSK/SDKDEFS/time.h     The SDK's time.h defines struct sgtbuf outside                         its include guard, and DEFS/SYS/types.h includes                         it by absolute path, so a second #include                         <time.h> defined it twice.  This guards it.  OSK/SDKDEFS/varargs.h  R. A. Larson's varargs, as in the SDK, with the                         member names made short.  tree.c has two va_arg()                         on one line, and with the long names that line                         is too long for this cpp, which dies on it.  DT_INET=9              stat.c names DT_INET, which this SDK's                         sg_codes.h lacks; 9 is os9lib's value.  SRC/COMPAT             supplies limits.h, assert.h and pwd.h, searched                         after OSK/DEFS.  std/stdc/memmove.c     memmove and vfprintf came from the porter's  std/stdc/vprintf.c     library; pdksh's own std/stdc versions are used.  std/stdc/strchr.c      built as index and rindex.  This C library's  std/stdc/strrchr.c     index(s, 0) returns NULL where pdksh expects the                         terminating NUL -- lex.c reads                         strchr(s, 0)[-1] -- and every built-in stopped                         with a bus error.  const= and NULL=0 let these                         K&R-compile.  OSK/SRC/setvbuf.c      left out.  It only changes a flag, and with this                         C library it left a FILE writing from a buffer                         that was never allocated; the library's own                         setvbuf is used.